.TH KBDECODE 2
.SH NAME
kbdecode, osklayout \- what was meant on a keyboard on a screen, and its layouts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "draw.m";
include "kbdecode.m";
include "osklayout.m";
kbdecode := load Kbdecode Kbdecode->PATH;
osklayout := load Osklayout Osklayout->PATH;

Lexicon.new:    fn(): ref Lexicon;
l.read:         fn(path: string, weight: int): string;
l.add:          fn(word: string, count: int);
l.lookup:       fn(word: string): int;

Decoder.new:    fn(lex: ref Lexicon, keys: array of Key): ref Decoder;
d.setkeys:      fn(keys: array of Key);
d.taps:         fn(t: array of Touch, n: int): array of ref Cand;
d.literal:      fn(t: array of Touch): string;
d.correct:      fn(t: array of Touch, c: array of ref Cand): string;
d.swipe:        fn(path: array of Touch, n: int): array of ref Cand;
d.learn:        fn(c: int, t: Touch);
d.offsets:      fn(): string;
d.setoffsets:   fn(s: string);

Layout.read:    fn(path: string): (ref Layout, string);
Layout.parse:   fn(name, text: string): (ref Layout, string);
l.place:        fn(r: Rect);
l.keyat:        fn(p: Point): ref Lkey;
l.dkeys:        fn(): array of Kbdecode->Key;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Kbdecode
turns touches on a keyboard drawn on a screen into words.  It knows
nothing of how the keyboard is drawn or where: it is given its keys, a
character and a rectangle each
.RB ( Key ),
and a word list, and answers with the likeliest words, best first, each
with a cost, a negative log probability.
.PP
A
.B Lexicon
is the words and how often each is used:
.B read
takes a file of
.I "word count"
lines (each count times
.IR weight );
.B add
one word.  Words of letters and apostrophes are kept, matched with no
regard to case or accents (é is typed with e's key); each keeps its
spellings, and the one shown is its lower-case one if it has one, else
its commonest
.RB ( I ,
.BR Dvorak ).
.PP
.B Taps
decodes one touch a character.  A touch is scored against each key by a
Gaussian round its centre and the offset learned for it
.RB ( sigma ,
in key sizes); the word list is searched as a trie, touch by touch, the
likeliest few hundred prefixes kept
.RB ( beam ),
a letter of the word not touched
.RB ( omit ;
an apostrophe all but free), a touch that is none of the word's
.RB ( insert ),
and two swapped
.RB ( swap )
each at a cost; and a word's commonness adds its own
.RB ( lmweight ).
Words longer than the touches are offered too, marked
.BR complete ,
at a cost for each letter more
.RB ( completion ).
A
.B Touch
whose
.B c
is not 0 is a character typed exactly, from a layer with no letters or
a long press.
.B Literal
is what the touches spell, the nearest key each.
.B Correct
is the word to put in for them, or nil to leave what they spell:
never one offered as a completion, never for a single letter or a word
with a digit; for a word not in the list, one likelier by
.B margin
(a word not in the list costs
.B oovbase
and
.B oov
a letter); for one in it, by
.BR validmargin .
.PP
.B Swipe
decodes a stroke through a word's letters: SHARK2 (Kristensson and Zhai,
UIST 2004).  Each word's template is the line through its keys' centres
(a doubled letter one key), so any layout works with no training.  The
words are pruned by the keys nearest where the stroke begins and ends
and by its length; the stroke and each template, resampled to 64 points,
are compared by shape (each scaled to a unit box,
.BR ssigma )
and by place (nothing while each stays within half a key of the other,
else how far apart, the ends counting most,
.BR lsigma ),
and the word's commonness
.RB ( slmweight ).
.PP
.B Learn
moves where a key's touches are taken to land towards where one did
(a touch meant for the key typing
.IR c );
.B offsets
gives what was learned as text, which
.B setoffsets
restores.
.B Setkeys
gives the decoder other keys, keeping what was learned for each
character.
.PP
.I Osklayout
reads a layout, rows of keys in text, and places them in a rectangle
(the format is at the head of
.BR /lib/osk/layouts/dvorak ).
An
.B Lkey
is a character's key
.RB ( Kchar ,
.B c
and
.BR shifted )
or one of the keyboard's own
.RB ( Kshift ,
.BR Kdel ,
.BR Kenter ,
.BR Kspace ,
.BR Kctrl ,
.BR Klayer ,
.BR Knext ,
.BR Kspecial ),
.B width
keys wide.
.B Keyat
is the key at a point, the nearest in its row.
.B Dkeys
are the keys a decoder works on, the letters' and the apostrophe's.
.B Alternates
reads
.BR /lib/osk/alternates .
.PP
.B tests/kbdecode_test.b
checks both;
.B tests/osk_accuracy.b
measures the decoder against a simulated typist on each layout, and
holds it to bounds.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/kbdecode.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/osklayout.b
.SH SEE ALSO
